			<div class="content" id="post-content-2668496">
									

<p>I'm a little iffy on this one. On the one hand, your focus on the personal impact was often well done (I enjoyed Vihaan and the doctor, the MTF officer being iffy based on what I'll get into below), but&#8230;</p>
<p>You've got a pretty clear-cut idea for an anomaly. I don't think your really sell it strong enough to push the horror of what it's like to face a thing that infects you due only to your traitorous thoughts, given the amount of page-length that comes after the explanation. Everyone just accepting their condition with dignity (or at least dedication) doesn't help this. You seem to be throwing out hints as to an origin (what I took to be a Fifthist reference in the palmistry book, the SCP logo), but they never seem to go anywhere. If you were making some alusion about what leads to the skin pattern, it went right over my head. (Edit: I also had no idea what was going on with the books. Am I missing something big here?)</p>
<p>So&#8230; well thought out, a well explained anomaly, and you've checked a lot of the boxes as far as thinking about implications and human costs, but for me it's a bit too pulled back. It feels like an ongoing slow-motion tragedy written from the point of view of an impersonal and timeless Foundation, real bland dystopian disregard for human life style. That's not generally a bad thing, but when you combine it with really dire measures, statements of its unpredictability, and origin hints that suggest that the Foundation is as much in the dark as we are, that level of detachment is just really unsatisfying.</p>
<p>I know that the SCP is that dire and those measures are called for, it just reads as a tragedy without an out/twist. Neutral vote.</p>

							</div>

								
