<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It used to. Note that there's no mention of any deaths in initial containment, how it's comparable to simple mass hysteria. Note how, in the 1995 log, less than 400 people have been infected in the sixtysomething years since they started counting, yet about fifteen years later there's almost 600 - and that's only the ones that live to be contained and given a number.</p>
<p>Keeping your hosts alive is all well and good when you can gently spread throughout a population, but what happens when you start getting cut off just days after setting off a chain reaction that took you years to muster the energy for? Why not simply take the energy you're dedicating to hosts that will never spread and instead use it to spark another start point in the hopes you won't be cut off again?</p>
